==================================================================
DAY 49 - PART B: LAB (WEEK 7 CHECKPOINT)
Build it all from code, verify, tear it all down, prove $0 running
Time: 120 minutes
==================================================================

GOAL
----
In one session: create the cluster, deploy your app from ECR with an
ALB Ingress, EBS-backed Postgres, S3 access via Pod Identity, an HPA,
then delete everything and prove nothing is left.


STEP 1: Put the whole setup in scripts
--------------------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks
Create up.sh that runs, in order:
  1. eksctl create cluster -f cluster.yaml
  2. k apply -f storage/sc-gp3.yaml
  3. metrics-server
  4. AWS Load Balancer Controller (Day 45 Step 6)
  5. your app: Deployment (ECR image, requests, probes), Service,
     ALB Ingress, HPA
  6. Postgres StatefulSet on gp3
  7. Pod Identity association for an S3-reading ServiceAccount used by
     your app's Deployment
Create down.sh that deletes in the RIGHT order:
  1. Ingress + LoadBalancer Services (wait 90 s)
  2. StatefulSets + PVCs (EBS disks)
  3. helm releases
  4. pod identity associations / iamserviceaccounts
  5. eksctl delete cluster --wait
  6. checks (Step 4)


STEP 2: Run up.sh and verify
----------------------------
[ ] curl http://<ALB>/ returns your app from several pods
[ ] k exec into an app pod: aws s3 ls s3://<bucket> works
[ ] Postgres data survives deleting pg-0
[ ] k get hpa shows a target
[ ] k get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone shows 2 AZs

Note the start time.


STEP 3: Run down.sh
-------------------
Note the end time. Calculate the approximate cost of the session
(hours x hourly estimate from Day 43).


STEP 4: Prove nothing is left
-----------------------------
REGION=ap-south-1
aws eks list-clusters --region $REGION
aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=instance-state-name,Values=running --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId'
aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --query 'NatGateways[?State==`available`].NatGatewayId'
aws ec2 describe-addresses --query 'Addresses[].PublicIp'
aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --query 'LoadBalancers[].LoadBalancerName'
aws elb describe-load-balancers --query 'LoadBalancerDescriptions[].LoadBalancerName'
aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available --query 'Volumes[].VolumeId'
aws cloudformation list-stacks --stack-status-filter CREATE_COMPLETE UPDATE_COMPLETE DELETE_FAILED --query 'StackSummaries[].StackName'
All should be empty (except things you intentionally keep: ECR repo,
IAM policies, S3 bucket if you chose to).

If a CloudFormation stack is DELETE_FAILED: usually a leftover LB or
ENI blocks VPC deletion. Delete it in the console and retry
"eksctl delete cluster".


